<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">İzmit</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Izmit, Nicomedia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">İzmit is a city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> at the head of the Gulf of İzmit on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>. As ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> it was capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> and the eastern residence of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was a port and shipbuilding town, where the exiled Hungarian rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> died in 1705. It is now an industrial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/izmit.jpg" alt="İzmit" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiki leylek · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Kocaeli_Sign_in_%C4%B0zmit_City_Center_01.06.2025_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>İzmit is the capital district of Kocaeli Province, on the Gulf of İzmit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>, about 100 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in the north-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The city is mostly built on hill slopes because of the cramped area, while flat plains surround the gulf. In antiquity it was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> and Ólbia, and the name İzmit derives from Nicomedia prefixed with the Greek word meaning to or into.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In antiquity the city was called Astacus or Olbia, said to be founded in 712 BCE. After its destruction it was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I of Bithynia</a> in 264 BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, and it remained one of the most important cities of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. Hannibal came to Nicomedia in his final years and killed himself at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyssa">Libyssa</a>, modern Gebze, between 183 and 181 BCE. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> was born there, and it was the metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>In 286 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> the eastern capital when he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>, and it remained the eastern and most senior capital until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> in 324. Constantine resided mainly in Nicomedia for the next six years, until in 330 he declared nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> to be Nova Roma, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He died at an imperial villa near Nicomedia on 22 May 337.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> in the late 11th century, but the successes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> returned it to Byzantine rule. After the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> recaptured it around 1235. The Ottoman Turks conquered it in 1337, and it became the capital of the Sanjak of Kocaeli. British forces occupied İzmit on 6 July 1920 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>, handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> on 27 October 1920, and the Turks retook it on 28 June 1921; atrocities against Greek civilians took place around the city in 1920–1921. The earthquake of 17 August 1999 devastated the region and killed more than 17,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>İzmit had 376,056 inhabitants in 2022. It lies on the main road and rail lines between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> and has a major port, and the Istanbul–İzmit area is one of the main industrial regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, with an oil refinery and paper and cement factories. Remains of the ancient city include the acropolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> and amphitheatre, Roman walls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>, and the Orhan Gazi Mosque of 1333.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “İzmit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thököly died in exile in Ottoman <b>Izmit</b> in 1705.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
